### Additional notes from phase 2

- **Locale-aware number and date formatting.** `toLocaleString('vi-VN')` / `toLocaleDateString('vi-VN')`
  were hardcoded in DataGrid, SqlEditor, App, SchemaMigration and the export/import dialogs. They now
  take `i18n.language`, so the thousands separator and the date format follow the active UI language.
- **`t` in a memoized callback.** In `DataGrid`, `fetchSchema` is a `useCallback` feeding an effect that
  clears the unsaved-edit buffer. Reading `t` directly would make it re-run — and wipe the buffer — on a
  language switch, so it reads through a `tRef`. `SqlEditor` uses the same ref for the Monaco action
  labels and the gutter run-glyph, which are registered once per editor mount.
- **A real bug the migration surfaced.** `SchemaMigration` drove its spinner off
  `busy.startsWith('Đang')` — a prefix match on a user-facing string. It now has a separate `working`
  boolean; the old check would have silently stopped working in English and Japanese.
- **More module-level helpers taking `t`:** `formatDuration()` and `gunzipToText()` in
  `ImportDatabaseDialog`, `formatRestoreEta()` in `ConnectionManager`. `IMPORT_FORMATS` in
  `ImportFilePicker` and `FORMAT_HINT` in `ExportDatabaseDialog` hold translation **keys** now, resolved
  with `t()` inside the component.
- **More `t` shadowing found and renamed:** `row` in `DatabaseInfoModal`, `vt` in
  `ImportDatabaseDialog`, `item` in `DatabaseInfoModal`'s tab list, `tab` in `DataGrid`'s import tabs,
  `name` in `SchemaMigration`'s diff list.
- **A second `exhaustive-deps` disable** was added to `DatabaseInfoModal`'s open/close effect, for the
  same reason as `ConnectionManager`'s.
- **`TerminalPanel` needs a `tRef`, not `t`.** Its shell-opening effect has `[epoch]` deps and writes
  into the xterm buffer from the PTY callbacks. Reading `t` directly would put it in the dependency
  graph, so switching language would tear down the live shell session. Same pattern as `DataGrid`.
- **Text already written into the xterm buffer is not re-translated** on a language switch — the
  buffer holds bytes, not React nodes. Only new output uses the new language. This is deliberate:
  rewriting scrollback would mean owning the terminal buffer, which the shell also draws into.
- **`RedisBrowser` has four components in one file** (`RedisBrowser`, `ValuePanel`, `KVTable`,
  `RedisConsole`, `RedisDashboard`); each needs its own `useTranslation()`. `KVTable` had to change
  from an implicit-return arrow to a block body to host the hook.
- **Two more `t` shadows:** `onChangeTypeFilter(t: string)` in `RedisBrowser` and the `cellText` local
  inside `printTable` in `TerminalPanel`. Neither was broken yet, but both would have silently hidden
  the translation function from any string added later in the same scope.

## Phase 3 — Non-component strings (DONE)

### 1. `src/utils/` — plain modules, no hook

`cryptoHelper`, `fileSave`, `redisDecode`, `xlsxReader`, `xmlParser`, `dumpPreview`, `exportHelper`,
`importPreview` now `import i18n from '../i18n'` and call `i18n.t('errors.…')` on the shared instance.
Namespace: `errors`.

`src/sql/statements.ts` needed no change — it throws nothing localizable, so it keeps its
"no imports beyond pure text helpers" property and stays testable in the node environment.

**The i18n bootstrap works under `environment: 'node'`.** The language detector no-ops without
`window`/`localStorage`, so tests resolve to `fallbackLng: 'en'`. Verified: importing `../i18n` into
`xmlParser.ts` left `xmlParser.test.ts` green. No test asserted on a localized error message, so
nothing had to be relaxed.

### 2. The positional-parameter key was a latent bug

`Tham số ? #N` was generated at three sites in `queryParamHelper.ts` and used as **the key of the
value map** — a map that is persisted in `localStorage` (`sql_query_param_values`). Localizing it
directly would have meant the extract pass and the substitute pass could disagree, and every saved
value would be orphaned on a language switch.

Fixed by splitting identity from presentation:
- `positionalParamKey(n)` -> `?#N`, language-independent, used by all three sites.
- `positionalParamIndex(name)` + `queryParams.positionalParam` build the label at render time in
  `QueryParamsModal`.

One-time cost: values saved under the old Vietnamese keys are ignored. `queryParamHelper.test.ts` was
updated to assert the stable key.

### 3. Rust backend errors — mapped at the `dbHelper` boundary

The real scope was **69 distinct strings** (≈55 genuine errors), not the ~280 lines quoted earlier —
that figure counted Vietnamese *comments* too. `"Chưa kết nối CSDL"` alone accounts for 26 call sites.

`src/utils/backendErrors.ts` maps them to the `backend` namespace:
- `EXACT` — 38 whole-message entries.
- `PATTERNS` — 21 regex rules that translate only the Vietnamese *framing* and pass the interpolated
  payload through, because that payload is usually a driver error already in the server's own
  language.
- Unmatched text is returned unchanged, so a missed or reworded message degrades to today's behaviour
  instead of being lost.

Wired in with **one** change rather than 63: `dbHelper.ts` imports `invoke as rawInvoke` and defines a
local `invoke()` that shadows it, translating `message`/`error` on the way out. Every existing
`await invoke(...)` call site therefore routes through it untouched.

> The wrapper rethrows a **string**, not an `Error`. A Tauri command returning `Err(String)` surfaces
> as a thrown string, and the `catch` blocks in `dbHelper` interpolate it directly (`${err}`) —
> wrapping it would have silently prefixed every message with `"Error: "`.

`dbHelper`'s own 18 Vietnamese literals moved to the `db` namespace.

**Why a table and not error codes in Rust.** Codes emitted by Rust would be the cleaner protocol, but
`cargo check` crashes in Tauri's build script on this machine (documented above in Commands), so a
103-site refactor of `src-tauri/` would have shipped without ever being compiled. The table is not the
"branch on user-facing text" mistake: these strings are produced by Rust in one fixed language and
never vary with the UI language, so they are protocol constants — the same relationship
`src/sql/statements.ts` has with `split_sql_statements`. **Reword a message in Rust and you must
update `backendErrors.ts`.** If you later want codes in Rust, that is a follow-up needing a working
`cargo build`.

`src/utils/__tests__/backendErrors.test.ts` (12 tests) guards it. The load-bearing one is the
**Vietnamese round-trip**: every `EXACT` message must translate back to byte-identical Vietnamese,
which proves in one assertion that the key exists, that `vi.ts` transcribed the Rust wording
faithfully, and that Vietnamese users see no behaviour change. It immediately caught a real merge —
`"Chưa kết nối database"` and `"Chưa kết nối CSDL"` mean the same thing and now share one key, so the
former is declared in `NORMALIZED_ALIASES` to mark the normalization as deliberate.

